<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Kish</h1><p class="st-meta">2900 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.) · capital Kish</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful city of northern Sumer whose name became a title of supreme rule: to be 'king of Kish' meant to hold hegemony over Mesopotamia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kish lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, east of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> names it as the seat of the first dynasty after the flood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enmebaragesi">Enmebaragesi</a>, who ruled around 2600 BCE, is the earliest king in the list confirmed by his own inscriptions.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kish had a mixed population, and its early texts show a strong Semitic element alongside Sumerian. Its kings exercised influence far beyond the city: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesalim-of-kish">Mesalim of Kish</a> acted as arbiter in the border dispute between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagash">Lagash</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/umma">Umma</a>, and later rulers of other cities, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum of Lagash</a>, adopted the title 'king of Kish' to claim wider authority. Its patron god was the warrior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zababa">Zababa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-zababa">Ur-Zababa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> rose to power and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> around 2334 BCE, absorbing Kish. The city survived as a cult centre under later rulers, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> and the Neo-Babylonian kings, and the prestige of its royal title outlasted it by centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 